# Node-RED Platform Reference [SPEC:NR-PLATFORM]

> Comprehensive reference for Node-RED's internal architecture, APIs, and conventions.
> Written as a verification surface for integration specs that make claims about
> Node-RED capabilities. Every assertion in this document is sourced from official
> documentation or core source code — not from inference or training data.

---

## 1. Runtime Architecture [NR-RT]

### 1.1 Core Execution Model [NR-RT-MODEL]

Node-RED is a flow-based programming runtime built on Node.js. The runtime
executes directed graphs of processing nodes connected by wires. Messages
(plain JavaScript objects) traverse wires from node outputs to node inputs.

**Key components:**

| Component | Role |
|-----------|------|
| Runtime | Node.js process that loads flows, instantiates nodes, routes messages |
| Editor | Browser-based SPA served by the runtime over HTTP; communicates via Admin API and WebSocket |
| Flows | JSON documents describing node graphs; stored in `flows_{hostname}.json` by default |
| Nodes | Individual processing units; each has a server-side JS implementation and an editor-side HTML definition |

The runtime and editor are architecturally separate. The runtime can operate
without the editor (`disableEditor: true` in settings.js). The editor
communicates with the runtime exclusively through the Admin HTTP API and a
WebSocket channel for live updates (node status, debug messages, deploy events).

### 1.2 Flow Structure [NR-RT-FLOWS]

A flow file is a flat JSON array of node objects. Every entity is a node —
tabs, config nodes, subflows, and working nodes all share the same representation.

**Core node properties (all node types):**

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `id` | string | Unique identifier, format `[0-9a-fA-F.]+` |
| `type` | string | Node type name (e.g., `"mqtt in"`, `"function"`, `"tab"`) |
| `z` | string | ID of the parent tab or subflow this node belongs to. Tab nodes have no `z`. Config nodes have no `z` (they are global by default) |
| `name` | string | User-assigned display name |
| `x` | number | Horizontal position in the editor workspace |
| `y` | number | Vertical position in the editor workspace |
| `wires` | array | Array of arrays. Each inner array is a list of node IDs connected to that output port. `wires[0]` = output 0's destinations, `wires[1]` = output 1's destinations, etc. |
| `d` | boolean | Disabled flag. When `true`, the node is present in the flow but not instantiated at runtime |

**Tab nodes** have `type: "tab"` and define a flow workspace. All nodes with
`z` matching a tab's `id` belong to that flow.

**Config nodes** have `type` set to the config node type (e.g., `"mqtt-broker"`)
and no `x`, `y`, or `z` properties. They are globally scoped by default.

**Subflow template nodes** have `type: "subflow"` and contain the subflow's
internal node definitions. **Subflow instance nodes** have `type: "subflow:{id}"`
referencing the template.

**Wire format example:**
```json
[
  {"id":"tab1","type":"tab","label":"Flow 1"},
  {"id":"n1","type":"mqtt in","z":"tab1","wires":[["n2","n3"]]},
  {"id":"n2","type":"function","z":"tab1","wires":[["n4"]]},
  {"id":"n3","type":"debug","z":"tab1","wires":[]},
  {"id":"n4","type":"mqtt out","z":"tab1","wires":[]},
  {"id":"broker1","type":"mqtt-broker","name":"Local Broker","broker":"localhost","port":"1883"}
]
```

This encodes: mqtt-in feeds function and debug in parallel; function feeds mqtt-out.
The mqtt-broker config node is referenced by mqtt-in and mqtt-out via a property
(e.g., `"broker":"broker1"`).

### 1.3 Message Passing Model [NR-RT-MSGPASS]

**Pre-1.0 (synchronous):** `node.send(msg)` directly invoked the next node's
input handler in the same Event Loop tick. Entire branches completed
synchronously before returning.

**1.0+ (asynchronous, current):** `node.send(msg)` queues the downstream
invocation via `setImmediate()`. Messages make equal progress through parallel
branches. This enables:
- Pluggable message routing
- Per-node timeout monitoring (individual node processing time, not cumulative)
- Fair I/O scheduling (sync code no longer starves Event Loop callbacks)

A temporary deprecation flag `runtimeSyncDelivery: true` in settings.js
restores synchronous behavior but is explicitly marked as non-permanent.

**Message cloning:** When a node sends a message to multiple outputs or multiple
wires, the runtime clones the message for each destination to prevent
cross-contamination. Nodes receiving a message own that object.

### 1.4 The `msg` Object Model [NR-RT-MSG]

Messages are plain JavaScript objects. No class, no prototype methods, no
required shape. Convention establishes these standard properties:

| Property | Type | Convention |
|----------|------|-----------|
| `msg.payload` | any | Primary data content. Nearly all nodes read/write this. |
| `msg.topic` | string | Routing/classification hint. MQTT nodes set this to the MQTT topic. Switch nodes can route on it. |
| `msg._msgid` | string | Runtime-assigned unique message ID for tracing. Automatically set by the runtime. |
| `msg.req` | object | HTTP request object (set by HTTP In node, consumed by HTTP Response node). |
| `msg.res` | object | HTTP response object (set by HTTP In node, consumed by HTTP Response node). |
| `msg.statusCode` | number | HTTP status code (used by HTTP Response node). |
| `msg.headers` | object | HTTP headers. |
| `msg.parts` | object | Split/join metadata for message sequences. |
| `msg.complete` | boolean | Signals completion of a message sequence (join node). |

**Critical convention:** Nodes should not construct new message objects from
scratch unless necessary. Building a new `msg` loses properties set by upstream
nodes (e.g., `msg.req`/`msg.res` needed by HTTP response nodes). Best practice:
modify `msg.payload` on the incoming message and pass it through.

### 1.5 Context Stores [NR-RT-CONTEXT]

Context provides persistent state storage outside the message flow at three scopes:

| Scope | Access Pattern | Visibility |
|-------|---------------|------------|
| Node | `this.context()` | Only the node instance that set the value |
| Flow | `this.context().flow` | All nodes on the same tab/flow |
| Global | `this.context().global` | All nodes in the entire runtime |

**Synchronous API:**
```javascript
var value = context.get("key");
context.set("key", value);

// Multiple keys (Node-RED 0.19+):
var [a, b] = context.get(["key1", "key2"]);
context.set(["key1", "key2"], [val1, val2]);
```

**Asynchronous API (for persistent stores):**
```javascript
context.get("key", function(err, value) { ... });
context.get("key", "storeName", function(err, value) { ... });
context.set("key", value, function(err) { ... });
context.set("key", value, "storeName", function(err) { ... });
```

**Store configuration** in `settings.js`:
```javascript
contextStorage: {
    default: "memoryOnly",
    memoryOnly: { module: 'memory' },
    file: { module: 'localfilesystem' }
}
```

Built-in modules:
- `memory` — in-memory only, cleared on restart
- `localfilesystem` — cached in memory, flushed to `~/.node-red/context/` every 30 seconds

Custom store plugins can be created implementing the Context Store API.

**Config node restriction:** Config nodes are globally scoped. They cannot
assume access to flow context — only node context and global context.

### 1.6 Environment Variables [NR-RT-ENV]

Nodes can access environment variables through multiple mechanisms:

- **Property substitution:** Set any node property to `${ENV_VAR}` in the flow editor. Runtime substitutes at flow load time. Limitation: cannot substitute partial strings (`CLIENT-${HOST}` does not work).
- **Function node:** `let val = env.get("FOO");`
- **JSONata:** `$env('ENV_VAR')`
- **TypedInput widget:** Supports `env` type for editor-side variable selection.

**Built-in variables (Node-RED 2.2+):**
`NR_NODE_ID`, `NR_NODE_NAME`, `NR_NODE_PATH`, `NR_GROUP_ID`, `NR_GROUP_NAME`,
`NR_FLOW_ID`, `NR_FLOW_NAME`, `NR_SUBFLOW_ID`, `NR_SUBFLOW_NAME`, `NR_SUBFLOW_PATH`.

**Subflow instance properties** are exposed as environment variables within the
subflow. Resolution order: subflow properties -> parent flow -> parent subflow ->
global settings -> process.env.

---

## 2. Node Development: Server-Side JS [NR-DEV-JS]

### 2.1 Module Structure [NR-DEV-JS-MODULE]

Every node's server-side implementation is a Node.js module exporting a single
function. The function receives `RED` — the Node-RED runtime API object:

```javascript
module.exports = function(RED) {
    function MyNode(config) {
        RED.nodes.createNode(this, config);
        var node = this;

        node.on('input', function(msg, send, done) {
            // Process message
            msg.payload = transform(msg.payload);
            send(msg);
            done();
        });
    }
    RED.nodes.registerType("my-node", MyNode);
}
```

**`RED.nodes.createNode(this, config)`** — Must be the first call in every
constructor. Initializes the node's base functionality (event emitter, context
access, status, logging). `config` is the node's configuration object from
the flow file.

### 2.2 Node Registration [NR-DEV-JS-REGISTER]

```javascript
RED.nodes.registerType(name, constructor, options)
```

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| `name` | string | Node type identifier. Must match the name used in the editor-side HTML registration. |
| `constructor` | function | Node constructor function. Called once per node instance when a flow is deployed. |
| `options` | object | Optional. Contains `credentials` and/or `settings` definitions. |

**Options object:**
```javascript
RED.nodes.registerType("my-node", MyNode, {
    credentials: {
        username: { type: "text" },
        password: { type: "password" }
    },
    settings: {
        myNodeColor: {
            value: "red",
            exportable: true
        }
    }
});
```

Settings follow a naming convention: node type (camelCase) + property name.
Accessed at runtime via `RED.settings.myNodeColor`.

### 2.3 Node Lifecycle [NR-DEV-JS-LIFECYCLE]

```
Flow Deploy
  |
  v
Constructor(config)             <-- node created, config available
  |-- RED.nodes.createNode()
  |-- register event handlers
  |-- initialize resources
  |
  v
'input' event (repeated)       <-- messages arrive
  |-- process msg
  |-- send(msg) to downstream
  |-- done() to signal completion
  |
  v
'close' event                  <-- flow redeployed or node deleted
  |-- cleanup resources
  |-- done() when cleanup complete
```

### 2.4 Input Event Handler [NR-DEV-JS-INPUT]

```javascript
this.on('input', function(msg, send, done) {
    // msg: the incoming message object
    // send: function to send messages downstream (Node-RED 1.0+)
    // done: function to signal processing complete (Node-RED 1.0+)

    // Backwards compatibility for pre-1.0:
    send = send || function() { node.send.apply(node, arguments); };
    done = done || function(err) { if (err) node.error(err, msg); };

    try {
        msg.payload = doWork(msg.payload);
        send(msg);
        done();
    } catch(err) {
        done(err);  // Reports error AND signals completion
    }
});
```

**`send(msg)`** — Routes `msg` to downstream nodes connected by wires.

**`send([msg1, msg2])`** — Sends to multiple outputs. `msg1` goes to output 0,
`msg2` to output 1. Use `null` to skip an output.

**`send([[msgA, msgB], msg2])`** — Sends multiple messages to output 0
(msgA and msgB separately) and one to output 1.

**`done()`** — Signals that message processing is complete. Required for
the runtime's timeout monitoring. If not called within 15 seconds, the
runtime logs a warning.

**`done(err)`** — Signals error completion. Equivalent to calling
`node.error(err, msg)` followed by completion signaling.

### 2.5 Close Event Handler [NR-DEV-JS-CLOSE]

Three signatures, selected by argument count:

```javascript
// Synchronous cleanup (no arguments)
this.on('close', function() {
    // cleanup synchronously
});

// Asynchronous cleanup (one argument)
this.on('close', function(done) {
    // cleanup asynchronously
    setTimeout(done, 1000);
});

// Full signature (two arguments)
this.on('close', function(removed, done) {
    if (removed) {
        // Node has been disabled or deleted entirely
    } else {
        // Node is being restarted (flow redeployed)
    }
    done();
});
```

The runtime enforces a 15-second timeout on `done()`. If cleanup exceeds this,
the runtime logs an error and continues.

### 2.6 Sending Messages [NR-DEV-JS-SEND]

```javascript
// Single output, single message
node.send(msg);

// Multiple outputs
node.send([msg1, msg2]);        // msg1 -> output 0, msg2 -> output 1

// Multiple messages to one output
node.send([[msgA, msgB], null]); // msgA and msgB -> output 0, nothing to output 1

// Suppress output
node.send(null);                 // no message sent
```

A `null` in any position suppresses output on that port. Messages are cloned
when sent to multiple wires on the same output.

### 2.7 Logging and Error Reporting [NR-DEV-JS-LOG]

```javascript
node.log("Info message");       // info level
node.warn("Warning message");   // warn level — appears in editor debug tab
node.error("Error message");    // error level — appears in editor debug tab
node.error(err, msg);           // error with originating message — enables catch nodes
node.debug("Debug detail");     // debug level
node.trace("Trace detail");     // trace level
```

**Critical distinction:** `node.error(err, msg)` (with the originating `msg`
as second argument) enables Catch nodes to handle the error. Without `msg`,
the error is logged but uncatchable.

### 2.8 Status Indicators [NR-DEV-JS-STATUS]

```javascript
node.status({fill:"green", shape:"dot", text:"connected"});
node.status({fill:"red", shape:"ring", text:"disconnected"});
node.status({fill:"yellow", shape:"ring", text:"connecting..."});
node.status({});  // clear status
```

| Property | Values | Description |
|----------|--------|-------------|
| `fill` | `"red"`, `"green"`, `"yellow"`, `"blue"`, `"grey"` | Status indicator color |
| `shape` | `"ring"`, `"dot"` | Ring = outline only, dot = filled circle |
| `text` | string | Short text (<20 chars) displayed beside the indicator |

Status updates are visible in the editor. The Status node (`type: "status"`)
can capture status changes from other nodes to trigger flows.

### 2.9 Accessing Config Nodes [NR-DEV-JS-CONFIGREF]

```javascript
function MyNode(config) {
    RED.nodes.createNode(this, config);
    this.server = RED.nodes.getNode(config.server);
    if (this.server) {
        // Access shared config: this.server.host, this.server.port
        // Access shared connection: this.server.client
    } else {
        this.error("Missing server configuration");
    }
}
```

`RED.nodes.getNode(id)` returns the config node instance by its flow-assigned
ID. Returns `null` if the config node doesn't exist or is disabled.

### 2.10 Credential Access [NR-DEV-JS-CREDS]

Credentials are stored separately from the flow file and encrypted at rest
using the `credentialSecret` setting in `settings.js`.

```javascript
function MyNode(config) {
    RED.nodes.createNode(this, config);
    var username = this.credentials.username;
    var password = this.credentials.password;
}
RED.nodes.registerType("my-node", MyNode, {
    credentials: {
        username: { type: "text" },
        password: { type: "password" }
    }
});
```

**Type semantics:**
- `"text"` — Value is accessible in both runtime and editor.
- `"password"` — Value is accessible at runtime only. In the editor, replaced
  by `has_password` boolean indicator. Never sent to the browser.

---

## 3. Node Development: Editor-Side HTML [NR-DEV-HTML]

### 3.1 File Structure [NR-DEV-HTML-STRUCTURE]

The `.html` file contains three `<script>` sections:

```html
<!-- 1. Node registration and definition -->
<script type="text/javascript">
    RED.nodes.registerType('my-node', { /* definition */ });
</script>

<!-- 2. Edit dialog template -->
<script type="text/html" data-template-name="my-node">
    <!-- form rows -->
</script>

<!-- 3. Help text -->
<script type="text/html" data-help-name="my-node">
    <p>Description for palette tooltip and help sidebar.</p>
</script>
```

The `data-template-name` and `data-help-name` attributes must match the
registered node type name exactly.

### 3.2 Node Definition Object [NR-DEV-HTML-DEF]

```javascript
RED.nodes.registerType('my-node', {
    category:      'function',              // Palette category
    color:         '#a6bbcf',               // Background color (hex)
    defaults: {                             // Editable properties
        name:   { value: "" },
        server: { value: "", type: "remote-server" },  // Config node ref
        count:  { value: 1, required: true, validate: RED.validators.number() }
    },
    credentials: {                          // Credential properties (editor-side)
        username: { type: "text" },
        password: { type: "password" }
    },
    inputs:        1,                       // 0 or 1
    outputs:       1,                       // 0+
    icon:          "file.svg",              // Stock or custom icon
    label:         function() {             // Dynamic label
        return this.name || "my-node";
    },
    paletteLabel:  "My Node",              // Override type name in palette
    labelStyle:    function() {
        return this.name ? "node_label_italic" : "";
    },
    inputLabels:   "input",                // Port hover label
    outputLabels:  function(index) {
        return "output " + index;
    },
    align:         'left',                 // 'left' (default) or 'right'
    button: {                              // Optional edge button
        toggle: "buttonState",             // References boolean in defaults
        onclick: function() { }
    },
    oneditprepare: function() { },         // Before edit dialog shown
    oneditsave:    function() { },         // On dialog OK
    oneditcancel:  function() { },         // On dialog Cancel
    oneditdelete:  function() { },         // Config node deletion
    oneditresize:  function(size) { },     // Dialog resize
    onpaletteadd:  function() { },         // Node type added to palette
    onpaletteremove: function() { }        // Node type removed from palette
});
```

### 3.3 Property Definitions in `defaults` [NR-DEV-HTML-DEFAULTS]

Each property in `defaults` is an object with:

| Attribute | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| `value` | any | Default value assigned when node is dragged to workspace |
| `required` | boolean | If `true`, node shows validation error when empty/null |
| `validate` | function | Custom validator. Receives value, returns boolean. `this` is the node instance. |
| `type` | string | Config node type name. Tells editor this property is a reference to a config node. Editor auto-generates a select dropdown + edit button. |

**Built-in validators:**
```javascript
RED.validators.number()         // Must be numeric
RED.validators.regex(/^[a-z]+$/) // Must match regex
```

**Reserved property names:** `id`, `type`, `wires`, `x`, `y`, `z`, `d`, `g`, `l`.
Exception: `outputs` may be included in defaults if the node needs configurable
output count.

### 3.4 Edit Dialog Template [NR-DEV-HTML-EDIT]

```html
<script type="text/html" data-template-name="my-node">
    <div class="form-row">
        <label for="node-input-name"><i class="fa fa-tag"></i> Name</label>
        <input type="text" id="node-input-name" placeholder="Name">
    </div>
    <div class="form-row">
        <label for="node-input-server"><i class="fa fa-server"></i> Server</label>
        <input type="text" id="node-input-server">
    </div>
    <div class="form-tips"><b>Tip:</b> Helpful guidance text.</div>
</script>
```

**Naming convention:**
- Regular node inputs: `id="node-input-{propertyname}"`
- Config node inputs: `id="node-config-input-{propertyname}"`

The editor automatically syncs `defaults` properties with matching input
elements by ID. Before the dialog opens, inputs are populated from current
values. On dialog close (OK), property values are read back from inputs.

### 3.5 TypedInput Widget [NR-DEV-HTML-TYPEDINPUT]

Initialized in `oneditprepare`:

```javascript
oneditprepare: function() {
    $("#node-input-payload").typedInput({
        type: "str",
        types: ["str", "num", "bool", "json", "msg", "flow", "global", "env"],
        typeField: "#node-input-payload-type"
    });
}
```

`typeField` references a hidden `<input>` that stores the selected type. The
widget renders a type selector button alongside the value input.

### 3.6 Multi-line Editor [NR-DEV-HTML-EDITOR]

```javascript
oneditprepare: function() {
    this.editor = RED.editor.createEditor({
        id: 'node-input-template-editor',
        mode: 'ace/mode/javascript',
        value: this.template
    });
},
oneditsave: function() {
    this.template = this.editor.getValue();
    this.editor.destroy();
    delete this.editor;
},
oneditcancel: function() {
    this.editor.destroy();
    delete this.editor;
}
```

### 3.7 Help Text Style Guide [NR-DEV-HTML-HELP]

Required sections in order: introduction paragraph (becomes palette tooltip),
Inputs, Outputs, Details, References.

```html
<script type="text/html" data-help-name="my-node">
    <p>Brief description used as palette tooltip.</p>

    <h3>Inputs</h3>
    <dl class="message-properties">
        <dt>payload <span class="property-type">string</span></dt>
        <dd>The input data to process.</dd>
        <dt class="optional">topic <span class="property-type">string</span></dt>
        <dd>Optional routing topic.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Outputs</h3>
    <dl class="message-properties">
        <dt>payload <span class="property-type">object</span></dt>
        <dd>The processed result.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Details</h3>
    <p>Extended description. Reference properties as <code>msg.payload</code>.</p>

    <h3>References</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="https://example.com">External API docs</a></li>
    </ul>
</script>
```

For multiple outputs, wrap in `<ol class="node-ports">`:
```html
<h3>Outputs</h3>
<ol class="node-ports">
    <li>Standard output
        <dl class="message-properties">
            <dt>payload <span class="property-type">string</span></dt>
            <dd>Result data.</dd>
        </dl>
    </li>
    <li>Error output
        <dl class="message-properties">
            <dt>payload <span class="property-type">string</span></dt>
            <dd>Error message.</dd>
        </dl>
    </li>
</ol>
```

Markdown help (Node-RED 2.1+) is supported with `type="text/markdown"`.

---

## 4. Configuration Nodes [NR-CONFIG]

### 4.1 Definition Pattern [NR-CONFIG-DEF]

Config nodes are distinguished by two conventions:

1. **`category: 'config'`** in the editor-side definition.
2. **`node-config-input-{prop}`** ID prefix for edit template inputs.

**Editor-side HTML:**
```javascript
RED.nodes.registerType('remote-server', {
    category: 'config',
    defaults: {
        host: { value: "localhost", required: true },
        port: { value: 1234, required: true, validate: RED.validators.number() }
    },
    label: function() {
        return this.host + ":" + this.port;
    }
});
```

```html
<script type="text/html" data-template-name="remote-server">
    <div class="form-row">
        <label for="node-config-input-host"><i class="fa fa-bookmark"></i> Host</label>
        <input type="text" id="node-config-input-host">
    </div>
    <div class="form-row">
        <label for="node-config-input-port"><i class="fa fa-bookmark"></i> Port</label>
        <input type="text" id="node-config-input-port">
    </div>
</script>
```

**Server-side JS:**
```javascript
module.exports = function(RED) {
    function RemoteServerNode(n) {
        RED.nodes.createNode(this, n);
        this.host = n.host;
        this.port = n.port;
    }
    RED.nodes.registerType("remote-server", RemoteServerNode);
}
```

### 4.2 Config Node as Connection Manager [NR-CONFIG-CONNMGR]

The canonical pattern for config nodes managing external connections (MQTT
brokers, databases, WebSocket servers):

```javascript
function BrokerNode(config) {
    RED.nodes.createNode(this, config);
    this.host = config.host;
    this.port = config.port;
    this.connected = false;
    this.connecting = false;
    this.users = {};           // Track dependent nodes

    var node = this;

    this.register = function(childNode) {
        node.users[childNode.id] = childNode;
        if (Object.keys(node.users).length === 1) {
            node.connect();    // Connect on first user
        }
    };

    this.deregister = function(childNode, done) {
        delete node.users[childNode.id];
        if (Object.keys(node.users).length === 0) {
            node.disconnect(done);  // Disconnect when last user removed
        } else {
            done();
        }
    };

    this.connect = function() { /* create client, attach handlers */ };
    this.disconnect = function(done) { /* cleanup, then done() */ };

    this.on('close', function(done) {
        node.disconnect(done);
    });
}
```

This pattern is used by the built-in MQTT broker config node and replicated
by most contrib packages managing persistent connections.

### 4.3 Referencing Config Nodes [NR-CONFIG-REF]

A regular node declares a config node dependency in `defaults`:

```javascript
defaults: {
    server: { value: "", type: "remote-server" }
}
```

The editor automatically renders a dropdown of available config node instances
plus a pencil button to create/edit instances. At runtime:

```javascript
function MyNode(config) {
    RED.nodes.createNode(this, config);
    this.server = RED.nodes.getNode(config.server);
    if (this.server) {
        this.server.register(this);
        // Use this.server.host, this.server.client, etc.
    }
}
```

### 4.4 Config Node Scope [NR-CONFIG-SCOPE]

Config nodes are globally scoped by default — the same config node instance
is shared across all flows. This means:
- Config nodes cannot access flow context (only node and global context).
- All regular nodes referencing the same config node ID share the same
  instance, regardless of which flow tab they are on.
- Config nodes are listed in the editor's Configuration Nodes sidebar panel.

---

## 5. MQTT Integration Internals [NR-MQTT]

### 5.1 Architecture Overview [NR-MQTT-ARCH]

The built-in MQTT nodes (`node-red-node-mqtt`, part of `@node-red/nodes`) are
located at `packages/node_modules/@node-red/nodes/core/network/10-mqtt.js`
and `10-mqtt.html` in the core repository.

Three node types form the MQTT subsystem:

| Node Type | Category | Role |
|-----------|----------|------|
| `mqtt-broker` | config | Manages a single MQTT connection. Shared by mqtt-in and mqtt-out nodes. |
| `mqtt in` | network | Subscribes to topics, emits messages into the flow. |
| `mqtt out` | network | Publishes messages from the flow to MQTT topics. |

### 5.2 MQTT Broker Config Node (`MQTTBrokerNode`) [NR-MQTT-BROKER]

**Underlying library:** `mqtt` (mqtt.js npm package) — the standard JavaScript
MQTT client. Supports MQTT 3.1.1 and 5.0.

**Connection state tracking:** Three boolean flags: `connected`, `connecting`,
`closing`. These control status propagation to all dependent nodes.

**Configuration options (via `setOptions()`):**

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| Protocol version | 3 (3.1), 4 (3.1.1), or 5 (5.0) |
| TLS/certificates | CA cert, client cert, client key, server name, reject unauthorized |
| Client ID | User-specified or auto-generated |
| Keep alive | Ping interval in seconds |
| Clean session | Whether to resume previous session subscriptions |
| Will message | Last Will and Testament: topic, payload, QoS, retain |
| Birth message | Published on successful connect |
| Close message | Published before graceful disconnect |
| Auto-connect | Connect on deploy or wait for explicit trigger |

**Subscription management:** Stored in `node.subscriptions` object, keyed by
topic. Each topic entry maps to the subscribing node instances. The broker
uses a helper function supporting MQTT wildcards (`+` single level, `#`
multi-level) and MQTT v5 shared subscription syntax.

**Event handler pattern:**
```
mqtt.connect -> set connected=true, publish birth message, update all node statuses to green/dot
mqtt.reconnect -> set connecting=true, update statuses to yellow/ring
mqtt.close -> set connected=false, update statuses to red/ring
mqtt.error -> log error, propagate to dependent nodes
mqtt.message -> route to subscriptionHandler()
```

**Graceful shutdown:** `disconnect()` publishes the close message (if configured),
then calls `client.end()` with a 2-second timeout before forcing cleanup. This
prevents hanging connections on flow redeploy.

**Custom listener tracking:** Methods `_clientOn()` and `_clientRemoveListeners()`
manage event listeners on the mqtt.js client to prevent listener accumulation
during reconnections.

### 5.3 Subscription Handler [NR-MQTT-SUBHANDLER]

When a message arrives from mqtt.js:

1. Extract QoS and retain flags from the MQTT packet.
2. For MQTT v5: extract `responseTopic`, `correlationData`, `contentType`,
   `messageExpiryInterval`, `payloadFormatIndicator`, `userProperties`.
3. Convert payload based on the subscribing node's `datatype` setting:
   - `buffer` — raw Buffer
   - `utf8` — string
   - `json` — parsed JSON object
   - `base64` — base64-encoded string
   - `auto` — attempts JSON parse, falls back to string
4. Match the incoming topic against subscribed topics (supporting wildcards).
5. Invoke each matching node's callback with the transformed message.

### 5.4 MQTT In Node (`MQTTInNode`) [NR-MQTT-IN]

**Static subscriptions:** When a topic is configured in the node properties,
the node registers with the broker config node at deploy time and creates
a permanent subscription.

**Dynamic subscriptions (when `inputs: 1`):** The node accepts `msg.action`
to control subscriptions at runtime:

| Action | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `connect` | Initiate broker connection |
| `disconnect` | Close broker connection |
| `subscribe` | Add dynamic topic subscription (from `msg.topic`, with optional `msg.qos`) |
| `unsubscribe` | Remove subscription (`msg.topic = true` to unsubscribe all) |
| `getSubscriptions` | Return current subscription list |

**QoS handling:** QoS values validated to 0-2 range, default 2. Node-level
and message-level QoS settings are both supported.

### 5.5 MQTT Out Node (`MQTTOutNode`) [NR-MQTT-OUT]

**Publishing pattern:**
1. Validate topic (from node config or `msg.topic`).
2. Convert payload: null/undefined to empty string, non-Buffer objects to
   JSON string, everything else to string.
3. Publish via mqtt.js client with QoS, retain, and (for v5) user properties.

**MQTT v5 properties forwarded:** `userProperties`, `responseTopic`,
`correlationData`, `contentType`, `messageExpiryInterval`.

### 5.6 Status Propagation Pattern [NR-MQTT-STATUS]

The `updateStatus()` function iterates all registered nodes and sets
consistent visual indicators:

| State | fill | shape | text |
|-------|------|-------|------|
| Connected | green | dot | "connected" |
| Connecting | yellow | ring | "connecting" |
| Disconnected | red | ring | "disconnected" |

This pattern — config node propagating status to all dependent nodes — is
the standard approach used by every well-built contrib package.

---

## 6. Editor APIs [NR-EDITOR]

### 6.1 Available API Namespaces [NR-EDITOR-API]

The editor exposes these API namespaces to nodes and plugins:

| Namespace | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| `RED.actions` | Register custom keyboard-triggered actions |
| `RED.events` | Listen for editor lifecycle events |
| `RED.notify` | Show notification toasts in the editor |
| `RED.sidebar` | Add/remove custom sidebar tabs |
| `RED.plugins` | Register editor plugins |
| `RED.editor` | Create code editors (Ace/Monaco) |
| `RED.validators` | Built-in validation functions |
| `RED.nodes` | Node registration and access |

### 6.2 Sidebar API [NR-EDITOR-SIDEBAR]

```javascript
RED.sidebar.addTab({
    id:           "my-custom-tab",           // Unique identifier
    label:        "custom",                  // Short label on tab
    name:         "My Custom Tab",           // Full name in sidebar menu
    iconClass:    "fa fa-database",          // FontAwesome 4 icon class
    content:      domElement,                // DOM element with tab content
    toolbar:      toolbarElement,            // Optional toolbar DOM element
    enableOnEdit: false,                     // Allow access during edit dialogs
    action:       "my-tab:show"              // Registered action for keyboard shortcut
});

RED.sidebar.removeTab("my-custom-tab");
RED.sidebar.show("my-custom-tab");
RED.sidebar.containsTab("my-custom-tab");   // returns boolean
```

**Timing constraint:** `RED.sidebar.addTab()` will fail if called before the
editor has finished loading. Plugins and nodes using `onpaletteadd` should
defer sidebar registration until the editor is ready.

### 6.3 Plugin Registration [NR-EDITOR-PLUGINS]

```javascript
RED.plugins.registerPlugin("my-plugin-id", {
    type: "node-red-plugin-type",
    // Plugin-type-specific configuration
});
```

Known plugin types:
- `"node-red-theme"` — Custom editor themes with CSS and optional Mermaid config.
- `"node-red-library-source"` — Custom library store backends.

### 6.4 Standard Widgets [NR-EDITOR-WIDGETS]

| Widget | Purpose | Usage |
|--------|---------|-------|
| TypedInput | Type-aware value input | `$('#el').typedInput({types: ["str","num","bool","json","msg","flow","global","env"]})` |
| EditableList | Dynamic list editor | `$('#el').editableList({addButton: true, removable: true, sortable: true})` |
| SearchBox | Filtered search input | `$('#el').searchBox({delay: 300})` |
| TreeList | Hierarchical data display | `$('#el').treeList({data: treeData})` |

---

## 7. Subflows [NR-SUBFLOWS]

### 7.1 Definition [NR-SUBFLOWS-DEF]

A subflow is a collection of nodes collapsed into a reusable single-node
representation. Subflows have:
- At most **one input** port.
- **Unlimited output** ports.
- **Environment variable properties** that can be customized per instance.
- An optional **status output** node for instance-level status reporting.

### 7.2 Subflow Status [NR-SUBFLOWS-STATUS]

Inside a subflow, a status output node can receive messages:
- Simple string payload: displayed as status text.
- Status object `{fill, shape, text}`: displayed as a standard node status indicator.

This allows subflow instances to show status just like regular nodes.

### 7.3 Packaging as Modules [NR-SUBFLOWS-MODULES]

Subflows can be packaged as npm modules via the Module Properties tab in the
editor, which manages name, version, and license metadata. This enables
distribution of subflow-based nodes via npm alongside traditional JS/HTML nodes.

---

## 8. Testing Framework [NR-TEST]

### 8.1 `node-red-node-test-helper` [NR-TEST-HELPER]

The official testing framework for Node-RED nodes. Built on top of the
Node-RED runtime itself, it instantiates a real Node-RED instance for each
test run. Uses Mocha as the test runner.

**Installation:**
```bash
npm install node-red-node-test-helper --save-dev
```

### 8.2 Core API [NR-TEST-API]

| Method | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `helper.init(require.resolve('node-red'))` | Initialize with Node-RED runtime location. Accepts optional `userSettings` object. |
| `helper.startServer(done)` | Start the embedded Node-RED server. Call in `beforeEach`. |
| `helper.stopServer(done)` | Stop the server. Call in `afterEach`. |
| `helper.load(nodeModule, flow, [credentials,] callback)` | Load node module(s) with a test flow and optional credentials. Callback fires when runtime is ready. |
| `helper.unload()` | Unload nodes between tests. Call in `afterEach`. |
| `helper.getNode(id)` | Get a node instance by its flow ID. |
| `helper.clearFlows()` | Clear all loaded flows. |

### 8.3 Standard Test Pattern [NR-TEST-PATTERN]

```javascript
var should = require("should");
var helper = require("node-red-node-test-helper");
var myNode = require("../my-node.js");

helper.init(require.resolve('node-red'));

describe('my-node Node', function () {

    beforeEach(function (done) {
        helper.startServer(done);
    });

    afterEach(function (done) {
        helper.unload();
        helper.stopServer(done);
    });

    it('should be loaded', function (done) {
        var flow = [{ id: "n1", type: "my-node", name: "test-node" }];
        helper.load(myNode, flow, function () {
            var n1 = helper.getNode("n1");
            try {
                n1.should.have.property('name', 'test-node');
                done();
            } catch(err) {
                done(err);
            }
        });
    });

    it('should transform payload', function (done) {
        var flow = [
            { id: "n1", type: "my-node", name: "test", wires: [["n2"]] },
            { id: "n2", type: "helper" }
        ];
        helper.load(myNode, flow, function () {
            var n1 = helper.getNode("n1");
            var n2 = helper.getNode("n2");
            n2.on("input", function (msg) {
                try {
                    msg.should.have.property('payload', 'expected');
                    done();
                } catch(err) {
                    done(err);
                }
            });
            n1.receive({ payload: "input" });
        });
    });
});
```

### 8.4 Key Testing Patterns [NR-TEST-PATTERNS]

**Helper nodes:** Use `type: "helper"` in test flows as message sinks. These
are lightweight nodes that emit `input` events for assertion.

**Injecting messages:** `node.receive(msg)` triggers the node's input handler
as if a message arrived from an upstream node.

**Method spying:** The test helper automatically instruments: `trace()`,
`debug()`, `warn()`, `log()`, `status()`, `send()`. Assert with:
```javascript
n1.warn.should.be.calledWithExactly("expected warning");
```

**Async spy events:** For asynchronous assertions:
```javascript
n1.on('call:warn', function(call) {
    call.should.be.calledWithExactly("bad input");
    done();
});
```

**Testing with credentials:**
```javascript
var credentials = { n1: { username: "user", password: "pass" } };
helper.load(myNode, flow, credentials, function() { ... });
```

**Testing config nodes:** Include the config node in the test flow array:
```javascript
var flow = [
    { id: "n1", type: "my-node", server: "s1", wires: [["n2"]] },
    { id: "s1", type: "remote-server", host: "localhost", port: 1234 },
    { id: "n2", type: "helper" }
];
```

### 8.5 Test Flow Construction [NR-TEST-FLOWCONSTRUCT]

Test flows can be constructed by:
1. Writing JSON arrays manually (most common for unit tests).
2. Exporting flows from the editor's Export menu, then replacing
   `"type":"debug"` with `"type":"helper"` in the JSON.

---

## 9. npm Package Conventions [NR-PKG]

### 9.1 Package Naming [NR-PKG-NAMING]

As of January 2022, new packages must use **scoped names**:
`@myScope/node-red-contrib-example`. The scope can be a user or organization
npm scope. Including `node-red` in the name aids discoverability but is not
mandatory for scoped packages.

Legacy unscoped names (`node-red-contrib-*`) are still accepted for existing
packages but discouraged for new ones.

### 9.2 `package.json` Node-RED Section [NR-PKG-JSON]

```json
{
    "name": "@myScope/node-red-my-nodes",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "description": "Nodes for interacting with FooService",
    "keywords": ["node-red"],
    "node-red": {
        "version": ">=2.0.0",
        "nodes": {
            "foo-in": "nodes/foo-in.js",
            "foo-out": "nodes/foo-out.js"
        }
    }
}
```

| Field | Required | Description |
|-------|----------|-------------|
| `node-red.nodes` | Yes | Map of node-type names to their `.js` file paths. Each `.js` file's `module.exports` function is called at startup. |
| `node-red.version` | No | Semver range of compatible Node-RED versions. |
| `keywords` | Recommended | Must include `"node-red"` for npm discoverability. Add only when the package is stable. |

**Node discovery mechanism:** At startup, the runtime scans `node_modules/`
for packages with a `node-red` section in `package.json`. It loads each
listed `.js` file and calls the exported function with the `RED` API object.
The `.html` file must have the same base name as the `.js` file and reside
in the same directory (or the directory specified in the path).

### 9.3 File Layout Conventions [NR-PKG-LAYOUT]

There are no strict requirements on directory structure. Common patterns:

**Flat layout:**
```
package.json
my-node.js
my-node.html
icons/
  my-icon.svg
```

**Organized layout (multiple nodes):**
```
package.json
README.md
LICENSE
nodes/
  foo-in.js
  foo-in.html
  foo-out.js
  foo-out.html
  icons/
    foo.svg
examples/
  basic-flow.json
locales/
  en-US/
    foo.json
```

**Icons:** Custom icons go in an `icons/` directory. Requirements: SVG or
PNG, minimum 40x60px, 2:3 aspect ratio, white on transparent background.
Font Awesome 4.7 icons can also be used: `"font-awesome/fa-automobile"`.

**Examples:** Example flows placed in `examples/` at the package root are
discoverable via the editor's Import menu.

**Locales:** Message catalogs in `locales/{lang}/{nodetype}.json` enable
internationalization of help text and property labels.

### 9.4 Local Development Installation [NR-PKG-LOCALDEV]

```bash
cd ~/.node-red
npm install /path/to/node-package
```

Creates a symlink. Node-RED discovers the package on restart. Changes to
the `.js` file require a Node-RED restart. Changes to the `.html` file
require a browser refresh.

### 9.5 Publishing [NR-PKG-PUBLISH]

Standard `npm publish`. After publishing, manual submission to
`flows.nodered.org` is required for catalogue listing (automatic indexing
was discontinued April 2020).

---

## 10. Popular Integration Patterns (Contrib Analysis) [NR-CONTRIB]

### 10.1 Pattern Summary from Three Major Packages [NR-CONTRIB-SUMMARY]

| Pattern | mqtt (built-in) | home-assistant-ws | influxdb | modbus |
|---------|----------------|-------------------|----------|--------|
| Config node for connection | `mqtt-broker` | `server` (HA instance) | `influxdb` config | `modbus-client` |
| Connection pooling | Yes (shared mqtt.js client) | Yes (shared WebSocket) | Yes (shared InfluxDB client) | Yes (shared serial/TCP) |
| Status propagation | Green/yellow/red to all users | Yes, via base class | None (gap) | Yes, via FSM states |
| Credential storage | `credentials` field | `credentials` field | `credentials` (username, password, token) | `credentials` field |
| Dynamic subscriptions | Yes (msg.action) | Yes (entity/event type) | N/A (query-based) | N/A (polling) |
| Error on `msg` | Via done(err) | Via node.error | `msg.influx_error` object | Via node.error |
| Close cleanup | Explicit disconnect with timeout | WebSocket teardown | Implicit (GC) | FSM-managed cleanup |
| TypeScript | No | Yes (73% TS) | No | No |
| Test framework | node-red-node-test-helper | Vitest | Standard Mocha | Standard Mocha |
| Node count | 3 (broker, in, out) | 20+ (entities, triggers, services) | 3 (config, in, out/batch) | 14 (client, read, write, flex-*) |

### 10.2 Built-in MQTT Nodes (`node-red-node-mqtt`) [NR-CONTRIB-MQTT]

**Source:** `@node-red/nodes/core/network/10-mqtt.js`

Architecture already detailed in Section 5. Key patterns to note for
integration verification:

- The mqtt-broker config node creates a single mqtt.js client instance.
- All mqtt-in and mqtt-out nodes sharing the same broker config get the same
  client. No duplicate connections.
- Birth/will/close messages provide connection lifecycle notifications.
- MQTT v5 properties (userProperties, responseTopic, correlationData) are
  fully supported and threaded through from msg properties.
- Subscription management tracks listeners per topic per node, enabling
  clean deregistration on node close.

### 10.3 Home Assistant WebSocket (`node-red-contrib-home-assistant-websocket`) [NR-CONTRIB-HA]

**Source:** `github.com/zachowj/node-red-contrib-home-assistant-websocket`

**Architecture:** Hub-and-spoke pattern.

- **Server config node** manages a single persistent WebSocket connection to
  Home Assistant. Handles authentication, reconnection, and entity state caching.
- **Functional nodes** (20+ types: events, entity state, service calls, triggers,
  sensors, switches, buttons) register with the server config node.
- **Three-tier integration hierarchy:**
  - `UnidirectionalEntityIntegration` — push state to HA, no feedback.
  - `BidirectionalEntityIntegration` — push state and receive commands.
  - `ValueEntityIntegration` — handle numeric/string value changes.
- **TypeScript** throughout (73% of codebase), compiled to JS for runtime.
- **Vitest** for testing instead of Mocha.
- **Node-RED context storage** used to persist HA entity registrations across restarts.
- **Credential handling:** HA access tokens stored via Node-RED's credential system.

### 10.4 InfluxDB (`node-red-contrib-influxdb`) [NR-CONTRIB-INFLUX]

**Source:** `github.com/mblackstock/node-red-contrib-influxdb`

**Architecture:** Config node + write/query nodes.

- **Config node** (`influxdb`) manages InfluxDB client, supporting three versions:
  - v1.x: uses `influx` npm package, `writePoints()` and `query()` methods.
  - v1.8-flux: uses `@influxdata/influxdb-client`, token = `username:password`.
  - v2.0: uses `@influxdata/influxdb-client`, explicit token credential.
- **Credential registration:** `username` (text), `password` (password), `token` (password).
- **Error pattern:** Errors attached to `msg.influx_error` with `statusCode` or
  `errorMessage`, then passed to `done(err)`.
- **Notable gap:** No `node.status()` calls — no visual connection state indicator.
  No explicit `close` handler — relies on implicit garbage collection.

**`package.json` node-red section:**
```json
"node-red": {
    "nodes": {
        "influxdb": "influxdb.js"
    }
}
```
Single JS file exports all three node types (config, in, out/batch).

### 10.5 Modbus (`node-red-contrib-modbus`) [NR-CONTRIB-MODBUS]

**Source:** `github.com/BiancoRoyal/node-red-contrib-modbus`

**Architecture:** FSM-driven config node + 14 functional nodes.

- **Config node** (`modbus-client`) uses `@xstate/fsm` finite state machine
  for connection lifecycle management (connecting, connected, failed, closed).
- **Protocol support:** TCP, UDP, Serial RTU, Serial ASCII, Telnet, C701.
- **14 registered nodes** covering read, write, flex-getter, flex-write,
  flex-connector, flex-sequencer, queue-info, response filter, server, and IO config.
- **Dependencies include:** `@openp4nr/modbus-serial` (runtime Modbus),
  `jsmodbus` (alternative implementation), `bson`, `debug`.
- **Status propagation:** FSM state changes propagate connection status
  (green/yellow/red) to all dependent nodes.

---

## 11. Admin HTTP API [NR-ADMIN]

### 11.1 Endpoint Summary [NR-ADMIN-ENDPOINTS]

| Method | Path | Permission | Description |
|--------|------|------------|-------------|
| GET | /auth/login | - | Get active authentication scheme |
| POST | /auth/token | - | Exchange credentials for access token |
| POST | /auth/revoke | - | Revoke access token |
| GET | /settings | settings.read | Get runtime settings |
| GET | /diagnostics | settings.read | Get runtime diagnostics |
| GET | /flows | flows.read | Get active flow configuration |
| POST | /flows | flows.write | Set (deploy) flow configuration |
| GET | /flows/state | flows.read | Get flow runtime state |
| POST | /flows/state | flows.write | Set flow runtime state (start/stop) |
| POST | /flow | flows.write | Add a single flow (tab) |
| GET | /flow/:id | flows.read | Get a single flow by ID |
| PUT | /flow/:id | flows.write | Update a single flow |
| DELETE | /flow/:id | flows.write | Delete a single flow |
| GET | /nodes | nodes.read | List installed node modules |
| POST | /nodes | nodes.install | Install a node module (by npm name) |
| GET | /nodes/:module | nodes.read | Get node module details |
| PUT | /nodes/:module | nodes.write | Enable/disable a node module |
| DELETE | /nodes/:module | nodes.install | Remove a node module |
| GET | /nodes/:module/:set | nodes.read | Get node set details |
| PUT | /nodes/:module/:set | nodes.write | Enable/disable a node set |

### 11.2 Runtime Configuration (`settings.js`) [NR-ADMIN-SETTINGS]

Key properties relevant to integration:

| Setting | Default | Description |
|---------|---------|-------------|
| `flowFile` | `flows_{hostname}.json` | Flow storage file name |
| `userDir` | `~/.node-red` | User data directory |
| `uiHost` | `0.0.0.0` | Listen interface |
| `uiPort` | `1880` | Listen port |
| `httpAdminRoot` | `/` | Root URL for editor and admin API |
| `httpNodeRoot` | `/` | Root URL for HTTP node endpoints |
| `disableEditor` | `false` | Serve runtime only, no editor |
| `credentialSecret` | (auto-generated) | Encryption key for credential storage |
| `contextStorage` | `{default:{module:'memory'}}` | Context store configuration |
| `functionGlobalContext` | `{}` | Objects attached to global context for Function nodes |
| `functionExternalModules` | `false` | Allow Function nodes to require npm modules |
| `mqttReconnectTime` | `5000` | MQTT reconnect delay (ms) |
| `adminAuth` | (none) | User authentication for editor/admin API |
| `logging` | `{console:{level:'info'}}` | Log level configuration |

