## 12. Key Architectural Constraints and Implications [NR-CONSTRAINTS]

### 12.1 Single Input Port Limit [NR-CONSTRAINTS-INPUT]

Every node has **at most one input** port. This is a hard architectural limit,
not a convention. A node cannot receive messages from separate logical channels.
All incoming wires merge into the single input. Differentiation must happen
via message properties (e.g., `msg.topic`).

### 12.2 Config Nodes Are Singletons [NR-CONSTRAINTS-CONFIGSINGLETON]

A config node with a given ID is instantiated exactly once, regardless of how
many nodes reference it. This makes config nodes the natural place for shared
connections and state — but also means config node bugs affect all dependents.

### 12.3 No Native Module System for Complex Nodes [NR-CONSTRAINTS-MODULES]

Node-RED nodes are loaded as Node.js CommonJS modules. There is no Node-RED-specific
module system, dependency injection, or plugin lifecycle beyond the constructor/close
pattern. Complex nodes must manage their own internal architecture.

### 12.4 Editor-Runtime Boundary [NR-CONSTRAINTS-BOUNDARY]

The editor and runtime are separate processes (browser vs. Node.js). They
communicate only through:
- **Admin HTTP API** — flow deployment, node installation, settings.
- **WebSocket** — real-time node status, debug messages, deploy notifications.
- **Credential masking** — password-type credentials never sent to browser.

Nodes cannot call runtime functions from the editor or vice versa. The
`oneditprepare` function runs in the browser; the constructor runs in Node.js.
Any data shared between them must go through node properties (stored in the flow
file) or the Admin API.

### 12.5 Deploy = Full Restart [NR-CONSTRAINTS-DEPLOY]

When a flow is deployed:
1. All nodes receive the `close` event.
2. The runtime loads the new flow configuration.
3. All nodes are re-instantiated via their constructors.

Node-RED supports partial deploys (modified nodes only, modified flows only),
but the mental model should assume: deploy destroys and recreates affected nodes.
Any state not persisted in context or external storage is lost.

### 12.6 Async Close Timeout [NR-CONSTRAINTS-CLOSETIMEOUT]

The 15-second timeout on `done()` in the close handler means nodes managing
persistent connections must implement non-blocking cleanup. The MQTT broker
node's 2-second forced disconnect timeout (well within the 15-second limit)
is the reference pattern.

---

## 13. Version and Compatibility Reference [NR-VERSIONS]

| Feature | Introduced In | Notes |
|---------|---------------|-------|
| Async message passing | 1.0 | `send`/`done` callback in input handler |
| Multiple context stores | 0.19 | `contextStorage` in settings.js |
| Environment variable substitution | 0.20 | `${ENV_VAR}` in node properties |
| Subflow instance properties | 0.20 | Env vars per subflow instance |
| Status node | 0.12 | Capture status changes from other nodes |
| Scoped package naming requirement | 3.x (Jan 2022 policy) | `@scope/node-red-*` |
| Markdown help text | 2.1 | `type="text/markdown"` in help script |
| Flow/group-level env vars | 2.1 | Set env vars on tabs and groups |
| Built-in `NR_*` env vars | 2.2 | `NR_NODE_ID`, `NR_FLOW_ID`, etc. |
| Global user settings env vars | 3.1 | Configure env vars in User Settings dialog |
| Subflow `NR_SUBFLOW_PATH` | 3.1 | Full subflow nesting path |

